// Every kind of declaration, named from outside what it allows.
//
// The module sits in a subdirectory so that `package` means something: it is
// a different package from this file, so `package` is refused here as well as
// `private`. The allowed side — a sibling naming a `package` declaration — is
// what `tests/programs/modules` covers.
//
// The binding cases are here because they used to compile. The modifier on a
// top-level `val` or `var` was parsed and then dropped, so `private val` said
// nothing while every other kind of declaration enforced it, and a file could
// not only read another file's private binding but ASSIGN to it. A modifier
// that is accepted and ignored is worse than one that is refused, because the
// file reads as though it were protected.
import "./visibility/shared.keal"

val a = secretFun()
val b = packageFun()
val c = SecretClass(1)
val d = packageRecord(1)
val e = secretBinding
val f = packageBinding
val g = PublicClass(1).hiddenField
val h = PublicClass(1).hiddenMethod()
val i = SecretEnum.One
secretVar = 2
